    {
      "short": "Envelope",
      "chapter": "technique",
      "title": "Keys inside keys",
      "body": [
        "For the notes themselves, Mendly uses <span class=\"term\">envelope encryption</span>. Each patient's notes are encrypted by the app with their own random <b>data key</b>. That data key is then encrypted — wrapped — by a <b>master key</b> held in a <span class=\"term\">key management service</span> (KMS), and stored next to the data.",
        "The master key never leaves the KMS; the app sends a wrapped key and gets it unwrapped only if allowed, and every unwrap is logged. A database dump alone is useless, and deleting one data key crypto-shreds that patient's notes."
      ],
      "changes": [
        "+ Key service holds the master key",
        "+ Per-patient data keys, stored wrapped",
        "~ Notes encrypted by the app"
      ],
      "metrics": {
        "inTransit": "yes",
        "atRest": "yes",
        "secrets": "source code"
      },
